<circuit version="1.1.0-SR2" rev="261109" stepSize="1000000" stepsPS="20000" NLsteps="100000" reaStep="1000000" animate="0" >
<description>
Window comparator: the LED lights when the input is outside the 1.67 V to 3.33 V window.

How it works:
- The divider (3 x 10k from 5 V) sets the window: high threshold 3.33 V, low threshold 1.67 V.
- U1 compares VIN with the high threshold, U2 compares the low threshold with VIN. Both are open loop (no feedback), on a single 0 to 5 V supply.
- The diodes OR the two outputs into the LED.

Input: 100 Hz triangle, 0 to 5 V (tunnel VIN). Scope: VIN, HI (U1), LO (U2). Speed 2 %.

What to try: change the middle resistor to make the window wider or narrower; change the input's amplitude so that it stays inside the window (the LED stays off).

Results [SIMULATED]: each output is high a third of the time (mean 1.65 V; theory 1.667 V).

Why it is here: a test circuit for the op-amp model (BH-032), for comparators without feedback. While developing the fix, these outputs went to -2300 V on average, because a waiting op amp was never called again.
</description>


